(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["mth-11-02"] = {"5.1":{"s":"A locus is the set of all points in a plane that obey a given geometric condition. The equation of a locus is satisfied by every point on the locus and by no other point. To find it, take a general point (x, y) and write the condition using x and y. Shifting the origin to a new point changes the coordinates of every point and the equation of the locus, but not the shape of the curve.","w":["A locus is a set of points; its equation must hold for every point of it and fail for every point not on it.","After shifting the origin, the shape stays the same: only the equation changes."]},"5.2":{"s":"A straight line is the locus of points for which the slope between any two of them is constant. The inclination of a line is the angle it makes with the positive x-axis, measured anticlockwise, between 0° and 180°. The slope m is the tangent of the inclination. The slope can also be found from two points on the line. Parallel lines have the same slope. A vertical line has no slope.","f":[{"x":"m = tan(theta)","n":"Slope of a line with inclination theta, theta not 90 degrees.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, 5.2, p.106"},{"x":"tan(theta) = (y_2 - y_1)/(x_2 - x_1)","n":"Slope through the points (x_1, y_1) and (x_2, y_2), with x_1 not equal to x_2.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, 5.2, p.106"}],"w":["The slope of a line parallel to the x-axis is 0; the slope of a vertical line is not defined.","Put the points in the same order in the top and the bottom of the slope formula."]},"5.3":{"s":"A line can be written in several standard forms, each suited to different given data. If the slope and one point are known, use the point-slope form. If the slope and the y-intercept are known, use the slope-intercept form. For two points on the line, use the two-point form. When the line cuts both axes at non-zero points, use the double-intercept form. The normal form uses the perpendicular from the origin.","f":[{"x":"y - y_1 = m*(x - x_1)","n":"Point-slope form: slope m, passing through (x_1, y_1).","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, 5.3, p.109"},{"x":"y = m*x + c","n":"Slope-intercept form: slope m and y-intercept c.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, 5.3, p.110"},{"x":"(x - x_1)/(x_2 - x_1) = (y - y_1)/(y_2 - y_1)","n":"Two-point form: the line through (x_1, y_1) and (x_2, y_2).","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, 5.3, p.110"},{"x":"x/a + y/b = 1","n":"Double-intercept form: x-intercept a and y-intercept b, both not zero.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, 5.3, p.111"}],"w":["Double-intercept form cannot be used for a line through the origin, or for a line parallel to an axis.","In the two-point form, take the same point first on both sides."]},"5.4":{"s":"Every straight line has an equation of the form ax + by + c = 0, the general form. From it the slope is −a/b and the intercepts can be read off. Two lines are perpendicular when the product of their slopes is −1, and parallel when the slopes are equal. The angle between two lines depends on their slopes. The distance of a point from a line, and between two parallel lines, has a standard formula.","f":[{"x":"a*x + b*y + c = 0","n":"General form of the equation of a line.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, 5.4, p.123"},{"x":"x*cos(alpha) + y*sin(alpha) = p","n":"Normal form: p is the distance of the line from the origin, alpha the angle of the perpendicular with the x-axis.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, 5.4, p.123"},{"x":"tan(theta) = |(m_1 - m_2)/(1 + m_1*m_2)|","n":"Acute angle theta between two lines of slopes m_1 and m_2.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, 5.4, p.123"},{"x":"m_1*m_2 = -1","n":"Condition for two lines of slopes m_1 and m_2 to be perpendicular.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, 5.4, p.123"},{"x":"p = |a*x_1 + b*y_1 + c|/sqrt(a^2 + b^2)","n":"Distance of the point (x_1, y_1) from the line a*x + b*y + c = 0.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, 5.4, p.123"},{"x":"p = |c_1 - c_2|/sqrt(a^2 + b^2)","n":"Distance between the parallel lines a*x + b*y + c_1 = 0 and a*x + b*y + c_2 = 0.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, 5.4, p.123"}],"w":["For the distance between parallel lines, first make the coefficients of x and y the same in both equations.","In the distance formula, keep the absolute value: a distance is never negative."]}};
